**Diagram to draw (large, front view, the left side of the heart on the right of the page):** a longitudinal section showing four chambers divided by the **septum**:

- **Right atrium** (thin-walled, top left of the drawing) receiving blood from the **superior vena cava** and **inferior vena cava**;
- **Left atrium** (thin-walled, top right) receiving blood from the **pulmonary veins**;
- **Right ventricle** (bottom left, wall moderately thick) leading to the **pulmonary artery**;
- **Left ventricle** (bottom right, the **thickest wall**) leading to the **aorta**;
- the **tricuspid valve** (right atrio-ventricular valve) and the **bicuspid (mitral) valve** (left atrio-ventricular valve), with **valve tendons** (chordae tendineae) attached to **papillary muscles** on the ventricle walls;
- **semilunar valves** at the base of the pulmonary artery and of the aorta;
- optionally the **coronary artery** on the heart wall.

**Direction of blood flow (arrows to be drawn):**

body $\rightarrow$ vena cava $\rightarrow$ right atrium $\rightarrow$ tricuspid valve $\rightarrow$ right ventricle $\rightarrow$ pulmonary artery $\rightarrow$ lungs $\rightarrow$ pulmonary veins $\rightarrow$ left atrium $\rightarrow$ bicuspid valve $\rightarrow$ left ventricle $\rightarrow$ aorta $\rightarrow$ body.

Shade or mark the right side (deoxygenated blood) differently from the left side (oxygenated blood). Give a title and label ruled lines.
